Fill the holes of one OpenArc sketch in TypeScript.

## Task
Fill the numbered holes «n» in the sketch of export const transpose. Everything else in the sketch is fixed and is checked to be unchanged (OARC-E099).

## Sketch (fixed; TransposeFn = (rows: string[]): string[])
(rows) => {
  const width = Math.max(0, ...rows.map((row) => row.length));
  const out: string[] = [];
  for (let c = 0; c < width; c++) {
    out.push(«1»);
  }
  return out;
}

## Holes
«1» one expression of type string: output row c: column c of the input read top to bottom, padded and trimmed as the instructions say

## Imports already in the file
import * as arc from "open-arc";

## Types (verbatim)
export type TransposeFn = (rows: string[]) => string[];

## Intent
Exercise "transpose": implement `transpose`. Given an input text output it transposed. Roughly explained, the transpose of a matrix: ```text ABC DEF ``` is given by: ```text AD BE CF ``` Rows become columns and columns become rows. See [transpose][]. If the input has rows of different lengths, this is to be solved as follows: - Pad to the left with spaces. - Don't pad to the right. Therefore, transposing this matrix: ```text ABC DE ``` results in: ```text AD BE C ``` And transposing: ```text AB DEF ``` results in: ```text AD BE F ``` In general, all characters from the input should also be present in the transposed output. That means that if a column in the input text contains only spaces on its bottom-most row(s), the corresponding output row should contain the spaces in its right-most column(s). [transpose]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transpose

## Calls allowed in fills
You may call: nothing (what the sketch itself calls). Any other call on an import or a port is rejected (OARC-E098); calls on locals and on functions a fill defines are fine.

## Effects allowed
none

## Allowed new imports
none

## Examples (deep equality; numbers match within 1e-9 relative and -0 equals 0 unless "exact"; the strings "$NaN", "$Infinity", "$-Infinity", "$undefined" stand for those values; {"$fake": name} is a test double for that parameter; {"throws": true} means the call must throw, and a "throws" string must appear in the error message)
{"args":[[]],"expected":[],"name":"Transpose > empty string"}
{"args":[["A1"]],"expected":["A","1"],"name":"Transpose > two characters in a row"}
{"args":[["A","1"]],"expected":["A1"],"name":"Transpose > two characters in a column"}

## Rules
Names the file already imports, and its same-file helpers, are in scope: call them directly, never re-import or re-implement them.
"imports" may name: specifiers under 'Allowed new imports', modules the file already imports, and the file of a type shown under 'Types' (marked 'not in scope').
For a default import use {"from": "x", "names": [], "default": "x"}.
Never use: process, globalThis, eval, Function, fetch, require, dynamic import(), setTimeout, setInterval.
Date.now()/new Date()/performance need effect 'clock' (Date.parse and new Date(value) don't); Math.random and crypto.getRandomValues/randomUUID/randomBytes need 'random'; console needs 'log'.
Deterministic output; no I/O except through parameters.



